# The reason this repo is on Bazel. An action sees the files it declared and nothing else,
# so a generator that loads a solid it did not name fails where it used to read a stale one.
build --spawn_strategy=sandboxed,local
build --sandbox_default_allow_network=false

# The interpreter and its packages are not in the workspace; the geometry is. A genrule's own
# PATH stops at /usr/local/bin, which is where node is and where rsvg-convert and blender are
# not — so an action that draws a print or a line-art SVG needs the brew prefix named too.
build --sandbox_add_mount_pair=/opt/homebrew
build --sandbox_add_mount_pair=/usr/local
build --action_env=PATH=/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin

# AN ACTION SEES THE ENVIRONMENT THIS FILE NAMES AND NO OTHER, which is the point — and the
# card deck's Chromium is reached by one variable. Unset on a machine whose browser is already
# where puppeteer looks by default, so this passes through rather than states a path: nothing
# changes here, and a runner whose Chromium was baked somewhere else can say where. Without
# it the deck dies on `Could not find Chrome` with the browser on the disk the whole time.
# `--check_up_to_date` re-invokes bazel with no command line of its own, so a flag that lives
# on one is a flag that makes every action read not-up-to-date to the run that carries them.
build --action_env=PUPPETEER_CACHE_DIR

# The kept work is not a declared input and not an output: `_boxes` and `_meshes` name an entry
# by the serialized shape it came off, and `_realized` by that plus the digest of everything the
# action holds. An action that finds one has been given the same thing twice.
# WHERE THIS CHECKOUT IS IS THIS CHECKOUT'S TO SAY. The interpreter every action runs is not in
# the workspace and an action runs in the execroot, so both are reached by absolute path — and
# an absolute path here is one machine's, one directory's, and no worktree's. `gen_build.py`
# writes `.bazelrc.paths` beside BUILD.bazel with the two flags that carry it, and `try-import`
# is the one place bazel expands `%workspace%`. A tree that has not written one builds nothing,
# and `gen_build.py --check` says so.
try-import %workspace%/.bazelrc.paths
build --action_env=HSM_NO_BUILD_LOCK=1

# A `.step.mesh` IS WHAT THE PAGE READS INSTEAD OF PARSING THE STEP, and it is in no `outs`,
# so the one an action tessellates goes when the sandbox goes. Sixty-eight generators export a
# solid and a hundred and three solids come out of a full build, each paying a tessellation
# nothing will ever read. The tree's payloads are kept by the runs that keep them — a hand run
# and the dev-server watcher — and neither sets this. A run that reads a payload IN the action
# writes its own: `render_scenes` calls `_write_mesh_payload` directly, for the viewer it stands.
build --action_env=HSM_SKIP_MESH_PAYLOAD=1

# BAZEL IS ONE SCHEDULER AND `_run_lock` IS THE OTHER, and what keeps them off each other is
# that everything outside bazel shares the lock: one CAD generator at a time, beside a build.
# An action cannot take that lock — eight of them each taking a global single-flight mutex is
# a build that runs one action at a time.
#
# SO BAZEL TAKES THE BOX, and a second build in this workspace waits on bazel's own lock rather
# than competing, so a build given fewer cores is not a lighter build — it is the same work
# holding that lock longer, with every other session queued behind it.
#
# AN ACTION IS NOT A PROCESS. Counting the two generators that start a browser undercounts it
# twice over: occt runs threaded inside one python, so FOUR actions carry a load of about
# twenty, and one puppeteer browser is nine processes and ~800 MB resident. This box is 8 GB.
# A full build sits in 3.4–4.2 GB of swap with a quarter of the machine in the kernel, and the
# same work costs 6170 s of action time at eight jobs against 3814 s at four.
#
# auto STANDS ON A READING TAKEN OF A BUILD THAT NO LONGER EXISTS, and that is the whole of
# what supports it. Full build: 652 s and 662 s at auto against 617 s at four and one arm that
# died on a render timeout. Six heavy CAD targets: 95 s and 118 s at auto against 97 s and
# 102 s at four. Peak swap moves 5%. Every one of those was measured while `//:render-scenes`
# held 92–98% of the critical path, which it no longer does — it redrew all 33 pictures on
# every execution until its png and sidecar were declared on both sides of it, and skips them
# now. A cap has not been measured against the build as it stands.
#
# AND MIND THE NOISE FLOOR BEFORE BELIEVING A NUMBER HERE: identical work on this box ranges
# 95–118 s on a ~100 s build, and a tracked file edited mid-run lands in the action through
# `cp -L` whether or not it was committed. A single run separates nothing that differs by less.
build --jobs=auto
test --test_output=errors
